---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const questions = [
  {
    question: "Is there a game where you guess internet speed?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Guess the Speed is a free browser game where players predict download and upload speed, run a real test against Cloudflare’s network, and compare their guesses.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is the speed test game free?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Guess the Speed is free to play in a modern browser, with no account, app install, or game cookies required.",
  },
  {
    question: "How many people can play?",
    answer:
      "A room-code game supports up to 12 players. For a local game, add players to one shared screen and pass the device around.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can kids play?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Kids can make simple download and upload estimates, lock them in, and see how close they were when the real test finishes. An adult can host and explain the numbers.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does it work on a smart TV?",
    answer:
      "It may work in a smart TV browser that supports modern JavaScript, but there is no dedicated TV app. A laptop or phone connected to a larger screen is often easier to use.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do you store our guesses?",
    answer:
      "Local game guesses stay in that browser’s storage on the device. Room games temporarily store names, faces, guesses, and scores on Cloudflare, then delete the room after six hours without activity.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is Guess the Speed a good filler game?",
    answer:
      "Yes. A round takes about two minutes and needs no setup, so it works as a quick filler between board games, while dinner cooks, or before a movie. Set the game to 1 round in Settings for the shortest version.",
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: questions.map(({ question, answer }) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    name: question,
    acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: answer },
  })),
};
---

<Base
  title="Speed Test Game: Guess Your Internet Speed with Family and Friends"
  description="Turn a real Cloudflare speed test into a free family game and two-minute filler. Guess download and upload speed together, then see who came closest."
  breadcrumb="Speed test game"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script
      type="application/ld+json"
      set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)}
    ></script>
  </Fragment>
  <main class="wrap content-page">
    <h1>The speed test guessing game</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Guess the download and upload speed before a real test reveals the
      numbers. Guess the Speed turns a familiar internet check into a quick
      family game, with points for the closest estimates. Play on one screen or
      use a room code to bring separate phones together.
    </p>
    <article class="content-card stk">
      <h2>What is a speed test game?</h2>
      <p>
        A speed test game gives the numbers a little suspense. Before the test
        starts, each player estimates how fast the connection will download data
        and upload it. Everyone locks in a guess, the browser runs the test, and
        the group compares those predictions with the measured result. You do
        not need to know the router password or understand every network
        setting. A reasonable hunch is enough to play.
      </p>
      <p>
        I built Guess the Speed from the tiny ritual that used to happen around
        our television: somebody asked whether the Wi-Fi was fast today, and
        somebody else wanted to guess first. Once there are guesses to compare,
        the needle moving across the gauge feels less like a utility screen and
        more like the reveal in a game show. My kids call me Appa, and I still
        hear that question before a test sometimes: “How fast will it go this
        time?”
      </p>
      <p>
        The game measures both download and upload speed. Download is data
        coming to your device; upload is data going back out. Those numbers are
        usually different, which means there are two chances to surprise the
        family.
      </p>

      <h2>How to play in about 60 seconds</h2>
      <p>
        If everyone is ready to make a guess, a round is easy to explain. The
        speed test itself usually takes under 40 seconds, although a big group
        may need a little longer to enter guesses.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Choose the players.</strong> On one device, add the people
          playing and pass the screen around. For separate phones, create a room
          and share its code or QR.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Guess two numbers.</strong> Each player estimates download and
          upload speed in Mbps. Use what you know about your plan, the room you
          are in, and how busy the home feels.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Lock the guess.</strong> Once everyone is ready, start the
          real test. Room guesses are hidden by the server from other players
          until the result is ready.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Watch and compare.</strong> The game tests against
          Cloudflare’s network, then calculates how close each player was and
          adds the round points.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Play another round.</strong> The default game has three
          rounds, and the host can choose other round lengths or endless mode in
          the settings.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The basic rules are on the <a href="/how-to-play/">How to play page</a>{" "}
        if your group wants to settle ties before the first guess.
      </p>

      <h2>Why guessing your Wi-Fi speed is weirdly fun</h2>
      <p>
        We tend to treat internet speed as a number that appears after the fact.
        Guessing first changes that. Suddenly someone notices that the game
        console is downloading an update, a video is streaming in the next room,
        or the laptop is farther from the router than usual. The estimate
        becomes a small prediction about the home everyone shares.
      </p>
      <p>
        The best part is that confidence and accuracy are not the same thing. A
        player can announce “definitely 500” and then discover the result is
        much lower. Someone else may quietly pick a modest number and land much
        closer. The game gives everyone a reason to watch the gauge and ask why
        the result moved, without turning the evening into a lecture about
        networking.
      </p>
      <p>
        The rules also make a very wrong guess less of a disaster. First,
        second, and third place award 3, 2, and 1 points, but place points
        require an average miss of 50 percent or less. A guess within 5 percent
        on download or upload earns one spot-on bonus point for that speed. A
        player can earn a bonus even when the overall average is outside the
        place-point cutoff.
      </p>

      <h2>One TV or everyone’s phone</h2>
      <p>
        For a living-room round, put one browser on a laptop or a screen
        everyone can see. Players take turns entering and locking their
        estimates on that device. The local game keeps its roster and scores in
        the browser on that device, so there is no account to create.
      </p>
      <p>
        When the family is spread around the room, create a room code instead.
        Each person joins from a phone, and the room server keeps other players’
        guesses hidden until the result. The host can choose which device runs
        the speed test. If a phone is assigned, that phone sends the live test
        progress while the host and other players watch the shared gauge. Room
        games support up to 12 players, use no accounts or game cookies, and are
        removed after six hours without activity.
      </p>
      <p>
        If your group wants a longer walkthrough, read{" "}
        <a href="/how-to-play/">the complete game rules</a>. For the difference
        between Mbps, MB/s, ping, and upload speed, the{" "}
        <a href="/internet-speed-101/">Internet Speed 101 guide</a> has friendly
        examples. You can also browse{" "}
        <a href="/family-game-night-ideas/">family game night ideas</a> or use
        the <a href="/internet-speed-lesson-for-kids/">classroom lesson plan</a>
        .
      </p>

      <h2>A quick filler game for family night</h2>
      <p>
        Not every game night needs a two-hour board game. Sometimes you have ten
        minutes while the pizza bakes, a short break between two longer games,
        or that slow stretch before the movie starts. Guess the Speed fits
        those gaps. A round takes about two minutes: everyone makes a guess,
        the test usually finishes in under 40 seconds, and the scores appear.
        There is nothing to set up, shuffle, or pack away afterwards.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the shortest version, open Settings and set the game to 1 round.
        You can also play one round as a warm-up before the main game, or keep
        a three-round match going between other games and crown the champion
        at the end of the night. The local game saves its scores in the
        browser, so nobody has to remember who was winning.
      </p>

      <h2>House rules to make it harder</h2>
      <p>
        The built-in game is about download and upload guesses. Once everyone
        knows the basics, you can add a side challenge with a pencil and paper.
        These are house rules, not settings inside the app, so agree on them
        before the test.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Room-hopping round.</strong> Run a round in the living room,
          then move to a different room for the next one. Keep the same device
          if you can, and compare how walls and distance seem to affect the
          result. Walk, do not run, and keep the router where it belongs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Guess the ping.</strong> Before pressing Start, write down a
          ping prediction in milliseconds. The game does not ask players to
          enter a ping guess, so compare your notes with the displayed result
          and award a house point to the closest prediction.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Closest without going over.</strong> Say out loud that a
          download guess only counts if it is at or below the actual download.
          The app does not have this alternate scoring switch, so keep the usual
          score and track this challenge on paper.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Teams.</strong> Pair a grown-up with a child, or make a
          parents-versus-kids round. The scoreboard still scores individual
          players; add each team’s points by hand after the result.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Is it a real speed test?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes. The game runs a real speed test in the browser against Cloudflare’s
        network. It measures ping, download speed, and upload speed, then uses
        the download and upload results to score the guesses. It is not a
        pretend number generator or a quiz with a fixed answer. Wi-Fi, other
        active devices, the test device, and the route to the network can all
        affect what the test measures. That variation is part of the
        conversation, not a promise that every test will match an internet
        plan’s advertised maximum.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a classroom version of the same idea, try the{" "}
        <a href="/internet-speed-lesson-for-kids/">
          30-minute internet speed lesson
        </a>
        . For a low-prep evening at home, the{" "}
        <a href="/family-game-night-ideas/">game night idea list</a>{" "}
        has room hunts, paper challenges, and a download-time guessing game.
      </p>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        {questions.map(({ question, answer }) => (
          <section>
            <h3>{question}</h3>
            <p>{answer}</p>
          </section>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="story-cta">
        <a class="go" href="/">
          Start a game
        </a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>
